not a pretty day on january 6 when the  brutality and the pain and the struggle  on display during that day did not end  on january 6th in fact in quick  succession in the days after  four police officers who fought that  violent mob went on to take their own  lives all in the wake of the attack  now in an exclusive interview with cbs  news the widow of one of those officers  is talking about the battle she waged in  the 14 months since her husband's death  scott mcfarland shows how it could  affect law enforcement families in every  american community scott this is a big  story good morning  tony good morning to you aaron smith  says her husband d.c police officer jeff  smith had no history of mental illness  or depression until he came home from  work from here on january 6.  jeff was  funny he was  a jokester d.c police officer jeff smith  had a family he loved a dog he loved and  his wife aaron told us a career he loved  being a police officer it was his  calling he knew that's what he wanted to  do that's what he should be doing he did  but he had no idea what awaited him on  january 6 2021 when he and his riot unit  raced to the capitol  body camera footage obtained by cbs news  reveals jeff arrived on the front lines  as rioter ashley babbitt was shot and  killed  while trying to breach a smashed window  near the u.s house floor they were in  the hallway and the first thing that he  could remember hearing was shots fired  how did he characterize it he  characterized january 6 as the worst day  of his life after hours fighting off the  mob aaron says jeff returned home in the  early morning with a black eye and in an  even darker mood i went to sleep and  then the next day his demeanor changed  and he was never the same what changed  he didn't want to talk  he was pacing at night  and during the day  sleeplessness sleeplessness the joke  stopped  the dancing around the house stopped  jeff went to the police medical clinic  the night of the attack to report a blow  to the head but notes from a follow-up  visit a week later reviewed by cbs news  do not show he was asked about his  mental health and then one night aaron  says she woke up to find him crying in  bed what did you think was going on  truthfully i wasn't sure  i tried to console him tell him  everything was going to be okay  i just told him that i loved him and  that  we'll get through whatever this is  nine days after january 6th jeff smith  took his own life one of four officers  who died by suicide in the seven months  after responding to the attack i think  the  physical attack on him  changed him and if he didn't go to work  that day  he'd still be here it was just the  beginning of aaron's loss like most  jurisdictions d.c did not consider any  suicide a line of duty death denying  aaron much of the family's income health  insurance and ceremonial honors  capitol police officer brian siknik  suffered fatal strokes the day after  january 6 ruled a line of duty death he  laid in state while aaron smith received  nothing no notes no recognition  nothing from the city nothing from the  department  and  it's hurtful  and it's sad that they can't even reach  out to  the widow of one of their own officers  it literally stops the pace stops 30  days after the death with the help of  attorney david weber smith decided to  fight dc on its decision weber said the  burden was on them to make the case  january 6th cause jeff's death  they say they found the proof in jeff's  body cam footage you see he's getting  jostled here he's getting jostled slowed  down weber contends the video shows  someone in the mob sees jeff's baton  allegedly striking him with it right  there it's gone now you're going to see  him fall backwards then there's this now  outside the capitol a metal pole sails  in from the crowd hitting jeff head on  he took shelter in a stairwell  there was a physical injury that took  place here we are able to prove that he  suffered a traumatic brain injury it was  more than a year until d.c agreed  earlier this month aaron received this  letter declaring jeff died from an  injury sustained while performing his  duties on january 6. why do you think it  took so long for the district of  columbia to determine that with a  suicide comes  a stigma and  something that the police department  doesn't want to face or recognize i  think she's a hero illinois democrat  tammy duckworth and texas republican  john cornyn say they hope this ruling  lays down a marker for the three other  officers who died by suicide after the  sixth it was a traumatic event and i  think it's entirely appropriate that the  law enforcement  officials be covered and for police  families nationwide the two senators  have introduced legislation to allow for  line of duty death designations when  police officers take their own lives  more than 750 officers have died by  suicide since 2017. the military has  made this pivot understanding that  injuries to the brain to the psyche is  just as important as an injury to the  body aaron smith is advocating for the  bill what do you think jeff would say  about this issue i believe he would be  proud of me right now i'm fighting for  my husband  but i'm also fighting for everyone else  who has gone through this as well  and it deserves  the recognition  if the legislation passes for officers  who die by suicide after january 1st  2019 there'd be eligibility for line of  duty benefits the d.c police department  and the mayor of the district of  columbia did not comment for our report  but as for erin smith she tells us she  has not brought herself to watch that  body camera video telling us quote  i relive it every day  live  scott uh aaron smith is a hero just as  her husband was tony you wanted that  yeah and i just want to add that you  know tammy duckworth senator duckworth  is absolutely right the military has  recognized this that there's such a  thing as physical  injury but also a moral injury that's a  term or a spiritual injury and it sounds  like the law enforcement if this bill  passes may take that view as well uh  which is what happened in this case  seems clear to me yeah um scott you're  reporting as always spectacular thank  you for sharing this important story our  heart goes out to those  who are all affected by that terrible  day thank you scott
 

